---
import { withBase } from "../../lib/paths";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { cases } from "../../data/profile";
const item = cases.find((caseItem) => caseItem.slug === "omni-agent")!;
---
<BaseLayout title="Omni-Agent" description="Omni-Agent case study by Tran Quoc Truong." >
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container page-hero-inner">
      <div class="case-hero-meta">
        <div class="case-index">{item.label}</div>
        {item.period && <div class="case-period">{item.period}</div>}
      </div>
      <h1>{item.title}: {item.subtitle}</h1>
      <p class="page-summary">{item.summary}</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn primary" href="https://github.com/truong51972" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View GitHub</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href={withBase("projects/")}>Back to projects</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tight">
    <div class="container content-grid">
      <article class="article">
        <!-- Overview -->
        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Overview</div>
          <h2>A project-centric AI platform with a clean control-plane / data-plane split.</h2>
          <p>Omni-Agent is an event-driven AI platform built as a Python monorepo. Everything is scoped to a <strong>Project</strong>, and a Django/DRF control plane owns the domain — projects, documents, jobs, and graph metadata — while a fleet of stateless workers executes the heavy technical work: OCR, chunking, and knowledge-graph analysis.</p>
          <p>The product direction is QA-focused document intelligence: turn requirement and specification documents into a queryable knowledge base, then use <strong>hybrid RAG + knowledge-graph retrieval</strong> to support test-case generation, change-impact analysis, and bug intelligence — applying my QA-automation background to an AI platform.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Architecture -->
        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Architecture</div>
          <h2>Five deployable apps and two shared packages.</h2>
          <p>The monorepo keeps each app independently deployable with app-local dependencies (no root workspace). The Django control plane is the single source of truth for domain state; data-plane workers are stateless executors that receive typed commands and return typed results.</p>
          <pre class="code-block">Omni-Agent (monorepo)
├── apps/
│   ├── omni-api            Django/DRF control plane (domain, jobs, auth, realtime)
│   ├── omni-doc-processor  Stateless Celery worker (OCR strategy, chunking)
│   ├── omni-docling        Docling document-conversion / OCR service
│   ├── omni-graph-analysis Knowledge-graph builder (LangGraph, networkx)
│   └── omni-web            React 19 + Vite console
└── packages/
    ├── omni-contracts      Pydantic DTOs, enums, worker command/result envelopes
    └── omni-worker-shared  Celery config, HTTP/S3 helpers, worker DI lifecycle</pre>

          <h3>Control plane (omni-api) owns:</h3>
          <ul class="clean">
            <li>Projects, documents, document versions, and graph metadata</li>
            <li>Domain rules, lifecycle transitions, and permissions (DRF, API keys, JWT)</li>
            <li>The generic PlatformJob control-plane for async work</li>
            <li>Artifact metadata and S3 storage keys</li>
            <li>Realtime updates over Django Channels</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Data-plane workers execute:</h3>
          <ul class="clean">
            <li>Document conversion and OCR via Docling</li>
            <li>Text chunking and committed-text snapshots</li>
            <li>Knowledge-graph build / validate / merge pipelines</li>
            <li>Embedding and retrieval tasks against the vector store</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- Knowledge Builder -->
        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Knowledge Builder</div>
          <h2>The first capability: versioned document processing.</h2>
          <p>Knowledge Builder is job-centric and version-centric. Each document carries a <code>DocumentVersion</code> lifecycle, and processing is split cleanly between control and data planes:</p>
          <ul class="clean">
            <li>Versioned uploads with explicit lifecycle states</li>
            <li>Docling-based OCR and document conversion in a stateless worker</li>
            <li>Chunking with the API owning the committed-text snapshot</li>
            <li>Processing artifacts recorded against the owning version and job</li>
            <li>S3-compatible storage with internal keys and presigned browser URLs</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The split is deliberate: the API creates the committed text snapshot and owns transitions, while the worker performs chunking and returns neutral artifacts — so a failed chunking run leaves the last ready version intact.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Knowledge Graph -->
        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Knowledge Graph</div>
          <h2>Hybrid RAG + graph retrieval for QA intelligence.</h2>
          <p>Beyond document chunks, Omni-Agent builds a project knowledge graph linking requirements, rules, test cases, and bugs. Combining vector retrieval with graph traversal gives the LLM both semantic recall and structural reasoning that pure RAG misses.</p>
          <p>The graph powers a set of QA-oriented use cases as the core product direction:</p>
          <ul class="clean">
            <li><strong>Change-impact analysis</strong> — a changed requirement surfaces both directly linked and semantically similar test cases.</li>
            <li><strong>Regression optimization</strong> — rank test cases by a learned risk score to run the highest-risk subset first.</li>
            <li><strong>Duplicate-bug detection</strong> — embed new bug reports and find near-duplicate candidates.</li>
            <li><strong>Test-gap analysis</strong> — link prediction surfaces requirements with weak test coverage.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The graph-builder pipeline (build → validate → merge → metadata export) is orchestrated with LangGraph and networkx, with the React console rendering the graph via Sigma.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Platform foundations -->
        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Platform foundations</div>
          <h2>Shared contracts and a generic job control-plane.</h2>
          <ul class="clean">
            <li><strong>PlatformJob</strong> — one generic job model keyed by project, job type, resource type, and string-backed resource id, so resources with different identifier types share one control-plane for user-visible async work.</li>
            <li><strong>Typed transport contracts</strong> — worker command and result envelopes live in <code>omni-contracts</code> as transport-only Pydantic DTOs, kept free of Celery, storage, or service logic.</li>
            <li><strong>Worker runtime helpers</strong> — <code>omni-worker-shared</code> centralizes Celery config, HTTP sessions, the S3 wrapper, and worker DI lifecycle, so the API never has to depend on worker-only dependencies.</li>
            <li><strong>Storage boundary</strong> — internal S3 aliases for backend IO, presigned external aliases for the browser; raw storage keys never leak past API/worker code.</li>
            <li><strong>UUID v7</strong> primary keys for document versions, treated as opaque strings across services.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- Stack & infra -->
        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Stack &amp; infrastructure</div>
          <h2>A production-shaped local topology.</h2>
          <p>Compose fragments wire the full system for local development:</p>
          <ul class="clean">
            <li><strong>Backend</strong> — Django/DRF, Celery, Django Channels, PostgreSQL, Redis</li>
            <li><strong>AI / retrieval</strong> — LangChain, LangGraph, Milvus vector DB, a LiteLLM + Ollama model gateway, Langfuse for LLM observability</li>
            <li><strong>Documents</strong> — Docling conversion service, MinIO S3-compatible object storage</li>
            <li><strong>Frontend</strong> — React 19, Vite, TanStack Query, React Router, Tailwind, Sigma/graphology graph visualization, ApexCharts</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The React console is a Google-Cloud-Console-like shell: a compact top app bar, an overlay navigation drawer, and slug-first routes under <code>/projects/:projectSlug/...</code>, with Documents and Knowledge Graph as the main project-scoped resource pages.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- ATA relationship -->
        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Agent Testing &amp; Operations</div>
          <h2>Where ATA fits in.</h2>
          <p>Agent Testing &amp; Operations is a durable product direction for Omni-Agent. That direction is now being built out separately as <a href={withBase("projects/ata/")}>ATA — Agent Test Agent</a>, an independent validation and audit MVP, with planned integration back into Omni-Agent as its evaluation module once the platform's backend support expands.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Technical Decisions -->
        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Technical decisions</div>
          <h2>Engineering trade-offs and design rationale.</h2>
          <ul class="clean">
            <li><strong>Control plane vs. data plane</strong> — Django owns domain semantics; workers are stateless executors. Domain logic never leaks into workers.</li>
            <li><strong>App-local dependencies</strong> — each app has its own <code>pyproject.toml</code> and lock file, so the API never pulls worker-only dependencies and apps stay independently deployable.</li>
            <li><strong>Transport-only contracts</strong> — shared DTOs describe payloads, not behavior, keeping the contract package framework-light.</li>
            <li><strong>Job-centric processing</strong> — every user-visible async operation is a PlatformJob, giving consistent status, observability, and resource linkage.</li>
            <li><strong>Concurrency safety</strong> — the document commit flow locks the current version directly and fetches nullable ancestor data separately instead of joining under a row lock.</li>
            <li><strong>Hybrid retrieval</strong> — graph traversal complements vector search to capture relationships that semantic similarity alone would miss.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Stack</div>
          <div class="tag-row">
            {item.tags.map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}
          </div>
        </section>
      </article>

      <aside class="sidebar">
        <div class="side-card">
          <h3>Role</h3>
          <p>{item.role}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="side-card">
          <h3>Evidence</h3>
          <p>{item.outcome}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="side-card">
          <h3>Capabilities</h3>
          <p><strong>Knowledge Builder</strong> — Built</p>
          <p><strong>Knowledge Graph</strong> — Building</p>
          <p><strong>Agent Testing (<a href={withBase("projects/ata/")}>ATA</a>)</strong> — Standalone MVP</p>
        </div>
        <div class="side-card">
          <h3>Shape</h3>
          <p>Python monorepo · 5 apps · 2 shared packages</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
